Z||000004||88formula||01-22-2001||06:33 PM||||Put the oil you were putting in before (should be 10w-30w) and try some of that oil additive that supposed to help lifter noises. When I worked for Ragsdale Pontiac Cadillac and Kia the Kia Sepias they had used to come in with oil that was too thin. It was like three in one oil. We had to change the oil to 5w-30w oil and run the engine above 2000 rpm for I think twenty minutes to get rid of the tappet noise they had as a result of the thin oil. You may want to try that and see what happens. Don’t run oil that is too thick in your engine because if it gets cold outside and you start your engine it will take longer for the oil to reach the engine causing more wear.  <P>I don't think you ruined it so don't worry.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Richard Parnell||01-22-2001||06:45 PM||||Thanks for the advice 88.  I already have run the engine at 2k and above on the freeway for some time, still the noise didn't go away.  I think we will need to drop the cradle to do an adjustment.  Here in Hawaii it doesn't get too cold.||||reg||14
Z||000006||Cooter||01-22-2001||07:44 PM||||The thin oil may have cut some crud lose and plugged the fill hole in the lifter. If so, some of the "mystery oil" remedies may be successful. If the lifter has truely collapsed, re-adjusting will not cure the problem.||||reg||1
